Output artifacts include a normalized rule set, matcher automaton, strategy tables, generated evaluator, and optional proof or trace of rewrites. Critical-pair and termination reports are analysis artifacts; neither is guaranteed merely because compilation succeeded.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Compile Shared Tests Without Changing Rule Choice}

\begin{verbatim}
rules := typecheck_patterns_conditions_and_replacements(source)
network := share_constructor_and_constant_tests(rules.left_sides)
while execution has not halted:
    matches := network.update(changed_terms_or_facts)
    enabled := evaluate_conditions(matches, current_snapshot)
    chosen := apply_declared_strategy_priority_and_refraction(enabled)
    atomically commit chosen replacement or production actions
\end{verbatim}

Legal optimizations include sharing repeated pattern tests, indexing by top constructor, precomputing pure conditions, and eliminating unreachable rules. Reordering rules is legal for a confluent strategy-independent system or when the declared strategy says order is irrelevant; otherwise it can change the normal form or effects. Folding a rule requires capture-avoiding substitution and preservation of matching modulo declared equations.

Validation checks variable binding, sort preservation, replacement construction, agenda metadata, and source-rule provenance. For pure terminating examples, compare source and generated normal forms and record the applied rule sequence when strategies are observable. For production systems, compare working-memory updates and ordered external actions. Critical-pair exploration can support a confluence claim but bounded testing alone cannot prove it. The preservation contract therefore names both the rule theory and its operational strategy.

\section{Expose a Critical Pair Before Sharing Tests}

Add two rules to a separate toy term system: \(f(0)\to a\) and \(f(x)\to b\), with no rules for \(a\) or \(b\). Both rules match at the root of \(f(0)\). A decision tree can share the test for constructor \(f\), but it must not drop one of the two matches unless the operational strategy says which one wins. There is no common reduct of \(a\) and \(b\), so arbitrary matching order changes the possible results.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Subject & Applicable root rules & Outcome \\
\hline
\(f(0)\) & specific and general & \(a\) or \(b\) without priority \\
\(f(0)\) & specific preferred & \(a\) with that priority \\
\(f(1)\) & general only & \(b\) \\
\(a\) & none & normal form \(a\) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
candidates(term):
    if constructor(term) != f: return []
    x := only_argument(term)
    result := [(general, {x_formal: x})]
    if x == 0: append(result, (specific, {}))
    return result

rewrite_at_root(term, strategy):
    matches := candidates(term)
    enabled := filter_conditions(matches)
    return apply_strategy_and_instantiate(strategy, enabled)
\end{verbatim}

The candidate order in that sketch is not the strategy. A deterministic first-rule strategy needs source-priority metadata; a nondeterministic relation can enumerate both results. When patterns overlap below the root, compare the two resulting terms after applying the same unifying substitution at the overlap. Additional rules may join them, but finding a shared reduct for this one example does not establish confluence for a larger system. Regression tests should distinguish one match, multiple matches without priority, and multiple matches with declared priority. That last case catches a matcher that returns whichever successful discrimination branch finishes first.

\paragraph{Do not reuse a match after its subject changes.}
In an incremental production system, a shared pattern network may
retain partial matches for facts that have not changed. When it
retracts a fact, it must also remove agenda entries that relied on
it, even if their terminal tests are cached. Include the matching
fact identities in each activation. To test invalidation, insert a
fact, form a match, retract it before the rule fires, and check that
no consequence occurs. This tests a different contract from root
pattern overlap: it is about the lifetime of evidence for a match.
